<section id="about" class="px-6 py-16">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl">
    <h2 class="font-display text-3xl italic text-accent-muted">About</h2>
    <div class="mt-6 space-y-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink/85">
      <p>
        <span class="font-display text-4xl italic text-accent">I</span> work on
        the Content Quality team at{" "}
        <a
          href="https://www.pinterest.com/"
          class="font-semibold text-accent underline decoration-accent/50 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-accent"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          Pinterest
        </a>
        . Most of my days go into the serving infrastructure and APIs that get
        ML models into production, and the experimentation tools that let our
        engineers test ideas quickly. It's the kind of work most people never
        see, but it shapes what 600M+ people find on Pinterest every month. I
        studied computer science at Stanford, with a focus on AI.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before that, I spent two summers at{" "}
        <a
          href="https://www.riotgames.com/en"
          class="font-semibold text-accent underline decoration-accent/50 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-accent"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          Riot Games
        </a>
        . One summer I built a simulator that let game teams test matchmaking
        changes before shipping them; the next, a system that catches offensive
        player names in real time across every Riot game.
      </p>
      <p>
        Wrestling has been a big part of my life for a long time. I wrestled for
        Team USA at the 2018 Pan-American Championships, and at Stanford I
        started the school's first women's wrestling club. Along the way I
        founded <em>Yes We Can</em>, a mentorship program that brought 90 girls
        from four local schools into the sport. I don't compete anymore, but I
        still coach whenever I can, and I make a point of stopping by my old
        high school and college wrestling rooms every time I'm home.
      </p>
